Hi Kalyan,

Thanks for your reply. I had a look at the previous link. I am sure this is not an issue of complete separation as decribed in the previous post from the link. This is because the regions which were calculated to be differentially methylated has a value of >20% but does not have any differentially methylated cytosines (DMC) identified in them. Furthermore, when I extracted the methylation values of individual samples at locations within the region, the control and treated samples were very similar with overlapping methylation values. As such, I am sure this is not an issue of complete separation. I am baffeled why these regions were considered differentially methylated  with a p and q value of 0 when cytosines in them are clearly not differentially methylated. I have attached an example of my data in the previous post: https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/methylkit_discussion/ZRXOEY7epxM. Please have a look at it. Any comments on this would be greatly appreciated.

