In addition, contexts are there for user information so people know what context they are looking for at a given time. If you look at the literature you will see that your co-called "artificial" discrimination between contexts are there for years,  that's not something we came up with. Please see any of the serious methylation papers on the subject, for example, lister et al. nature paper from 2008 and many more you can find via PubMed. non-CpG methylation is probably maintained by different mechanisms than CpG mechanism (some papers indicate so), I suggest you do a basic literature search before forming opinions on context and why there are contexts, why they should be treated separately.

Best,
Altuna

